Write a program that takes a string A and two integers, wsize and lsize, as input. The program should implement a function that finds all occurrences of substrings of length wsize in A, where the substrings are formed by taking lsize consecutive chunks of wsize characters each. The function should return the count of unique substrings found.

For example, given the input string A = "abracadabra", wsize = 2, and lsize = 3, the function should find all unique substrings of length 2 formed by taking 3 consecutive chunks. In this case, the chunks would be "abr", "aca", and "dab", and the unique substrings of length 2 formed from these chunks are "ab", "br", "ac", "ca", "da", and "ab". Therefore, the function should return 6.

Write a program to solve this problem.

### Solution:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <string>

int countUniqueSubstrings(const std::string& A, int wsize, int lsize) {
    int count = 0;
    std::unordered_map<std::string, int> uniqueSubstrings;

    for (int i = 0; (i + wsize * lsize - 1) < A.size(); ++i) {
        std::unordered_map<std::string, int> tempMap;
        int j = 0;
        while (j < lsize) {
            std::string temp = A.substr(i + j * wsize, wsize);
            tempMap[temp]++;
            if (tempMap[temp] == 1) {
                uniqueSubstrings[temp]++;
            }
            j++;
        }
    }

    return uniqueSubstrings.size();
}

