Your task is to implement a ring buffer with the following functionalities:
1. `new(size: usize)`: Create a new ring buffer with the specified size.
2. `is_full() -> bool`: Check if the ring buffer is full.
3. `is_empty() -> bool`: Check if the ring buffer is empty.
4. `enqueue(item: T) -> Result<(), &'static str>`: Add an item to the ring buffer. If the buffer is full, return an error message.
5. `dequeue() -> Option<T>`: Remove and return an item from the ring buffer. If the buffer is empty, return `None`.

You should also implement a simple test to verify the correctness of your ring buffer implementation.

### Solution:
```rust
struct RingBuffer<T> {
    buffer: Vec<Option<T>>,
    size: usize,
    head: usize,
    tail: usize,
}

impl<T> RingBuffer<T> {
    fn new(size: usize) -> Self {
        RingBuffer {
            buffer: vec![None; size],
            size,
            head: 0,
            tail: 0,
        }
    }

    fn is_full(&self) -> bool {
        (self.tail + 1) % self.size == self.head
    }

    fn is_empty(&self) -> bool {
        self.head == self.tail
    }

    fn enqueue(&mut self, item: T) -> Result<(), &'static str> {
        if self.is_full() {
            Err("Buffer is full")
        } else {
            self.buffer[self.tail] = Some(item);
            self.tail = (self.tail + 1) % self.size;
            Ok(())
        }
    }

    fn dequeue(&mut self) -> Option<T> {
        if self.is_empty() {
            None
        } else {
            let item = self.buffer[self.head].take();
            self.head = (self.head + 1) % self.size;
            item
        }
    }
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn test_ring_buffer() {
        let mut rb = RingBuffer::new(3);
        assert!(rb.is_empty());
        assert!(!rb.is_full());

        rb.enqueue(1).unwrap();
        rb.enqueue(2).unwrap();
        rb.enqueue(3).unwrap();
        assert!(rb.is_full());

        assert_eq!(rb.dequeue(), Some(1));
        assert_eq!(rb.dequeue(), Some(2));
        assert_eq!(rb.dequeue(), Some(3));
        assert!(rb.is_empty());
    }
}
```

This solution provides a Rust implementation of a ring buffer with the specified functionalities. The `RingBuffer` struct maintains a vector of optional items, along with indices for head and tail positions. The methods `is_full`, `is_empty`, `enqueue`, and `dequeue` are implemented to handle the buffer operations. Additionally, a test case is included to verify the correctness of the ring buffer implementation.

