You are tasked with implementing a function that will parse a given HTML file and count the occurrences of specific HTML comments within it. HTML comments are defined within `<!--` and `-->` tags. Your function should take the HTML file content as input and return the count of the specified HTML comments.

Function Signature: `int countHTMLComments(String htmlContent, String targetComment)`

Input:
- `htmlContent`: A string representing the content of the HTML file.
- `targetComment`: A string representing the specific HTML comment to count within the HTML content.

Output:
- An integer representing the count of occurrences of the `targetComment` within the `htmlContent`.

Example:
```java
String htmlContent = "<html>\n<head>\n</head>\n<body>\n<!-- This is a comment -->\n<div>\n<!-- Another comment -->\n</div>\n<!--*****END ACTION CALL*****---->\n@endif\n</body>\n</html>";
String targetComment = "Another comment";
int count = countHTMLComments(htmlContent, targetComment);
// count should be 1
```

### Solution:
```java
public static int countHTMLComments(String htmlContent, String targetComment) {
    int count = 0;
    int startIndex = 0;
    while (startIndex < htmlContent.length()) {
        int commentStart = htmlContent.indexOf("<!--", startIndex);
        if (commentStart == -1) {
            break;
        }
        int commentEnd = htmlContent.indexOf("-->", commentStart);
        if (commentEnd == -1) {
            break;
        }
        String comment = htmlContent.substring(commentStart + 4, commentEnd).trim();
        if (comment.equals(targetComment)) {
            count++;
        }
        startIndex = commentEnd + 3;
    }
    return count;
}
```

The provided solution defines a function `countHTMLComments` that takes the HTML content and the target comment as input. It then iterates through the HTML content, searching for HTML comment tags `<!--` and `-->`. For each comment found, it extracts the comment content and compares it with the target comment. If a match is found, the count is incremented. Finally, the function returns the total count of the specified HTML comment within the HTML content.

