    print("Rotate");
    rotating = true;
    // Your implementation for array rotation goes here
}
```

Your implementation should handle the following cases:
- If the input array is empty, the rotation should have no effect.
- If the rotation count is greater than the length of the array, the rotation should wrap around and continue from the beginning of the array.

Your task is to complete the implementation of the array rotation algorithm within the given code snippet.

### Solution:
```java
{
    print("Rotate");
    rotating = true;

    int[] array = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}; // Example input array
    int rotationCount = 2; // Example rotation count

    int n = array.length;
    rotationCount = rotationCount % n; // Normalize rotation count if it exceeds array length

    if (n > 0) {
        reverse(array, 0, n - 1); // Reverse the entire array
        reverse(array, 0, rotationCount - 1); // Reverse the first part of the array
        reverse(array, rotationCount, n - 1); // Reverse the second part of the array
    }

    // Helper function to reverse a portion of the array in-place
    void reverse(int[] arr, int start, int end) {
        while (start < end) {
            int temp = arr[start];
            arr[start] = arr[end];
            arr[end] = temp;
            start++;
            end--;
        }
    }

    // The array 'array' is now rotated by 'rotationCount' positions to the right
}
```

In the solution, we first normalize the rotation count to ensure it is within the range of the array length. Then, we use the three-step reversal algorithm to perform the rotation in-place. The array is reversed as a whole, then the first part and the second part are reversed separately, effectively achieving the desired rotation.

