```

For example, given the input array `[3, 7, 2, 3, 8, 7, 9, 2]`, the function should return `4` because there are 4 unique elements in the array: 8, 9, 3, and 2.

You should ensure that your implementation has a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(n).

### Solution:
```c++
#include <unordered_map>

int countUniqueElements(int arr[], int size) {
    std::unordered_map<int, int> frequencyMap;
    int uniqueCount = 0;

    // Count the frequency of each element in the array
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        frequencyMap[arr[i]]++;
    }

    // Count the number of unique elements
    for (const auto& pair : frequencyMap) {
        if (pair.second == 1) {
            uniqueCount++;
        }
    }

    return uniqueCount;
}
```
In this solution, we use an unordered map to store the frequency of each element in the input array. Then, we iterate through the map to count the number of unique elements (i.e., elements with a frequency of 1). This approach ensures a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(n), where n is the size of the input array.

