```
The function should take an unsigned 32-bit integer `num` and an `Endianness` enum value `endianness`, and return the converted integer based on the specified endianness.

For example, if `num` is `0x12345678` and `endianness` is `wkbXDR` (big-endian), the function should return `0x78563412`. If `endianness` is `wkbNDR` (little-endian), the function should return `0x78563412`.

Assume that the input `num` will always be a valid 32-bit unsigned integer.

Write the function `convertEndianness` to accomplish this task.

### Solution:
```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint32_t convertEndianness(uint32_t num, enum Endianness endianness) {
    if (endianness == wkbXDR) {  // Big Endian
        return ((num >> 24) & 0xFF) | ((num >> 8) & 0xFF00) | ((num << 8) & 0xFF0000) | ((num << 24) & 0xFF000000);
    } else {  // Little Endian
        return ((num << 24) & 0xFF000000) | ((num << 8) & 0xFF0000) | ((num >> 8) & 0xFF00) | ((num >> 24) & 0xFF);
    }
}
```

The `convertEndianness` function takes the input `num` and the specified endianness. If the endianness is big-endian, it rearranges the bytes of `num` to convert it to big-endian format. If the endianness is little-endian, it rearranges the bytes to convert it to little-endian format. The function then returns the converted integer.

