```

Your implementation should make use of SIMD instructions to compare the input character `c` with the characters in `char_set` in parallel, and return true if any of the characters in `char_set` match `c`.

You can assume that the input character set `char_set` is a compile-time constant and does not exceed a certain size (e.g., 16 characters).

Your solution should be efficient and take advantage of SIMD parallelism to perform the character set matching.

### Solution:
```cpp
#include <immintrin.h>

constexpr bool simd_char_match(char c, const char* char_set, size_t set_size) {
    __m128i input = _mm_set1_epi8(c);  // Broadcast input character to all lanes of the SIMD register
    size_t i = 0;
    for (; i + 16 <= set_size; i += 16) {
        __m128i chunk = _mm_loadu_si128(reinterpret_cast<const __m128i*>(char_set + i));  // Load 16 characters from char_set
        __m128i result = _mm_cmpeq_epi8(input, chunk);  // Compare input with the chunk
        int mask = _mm_movemask_epi8(result);  // Generate a mask of matching positions
        if (mask != 0) {
            return true;  // If any match is found, return true
        }
    }
    for (; i < set_size; ++i) {
        if (char_set[i] == c) {
            return true;  // Linear search for any remaining characters
        }
    }
    return false;  // No match found
}
```

In the solution, we use the SSE2 SIMD instructions to efficiently compare the input character with the characters in the `char_set`. The function processes the characters in `char_set` in chunks of 16 characters at a time, using SIMD parallelism to compare them with the input character. If any match is found, the function returns true. If no match is found, it falls back to a linear search for any remaining characters in the set. This approach leverages SIMD parallelism to efficiently perform character set matching.

